        },
        {
          "metric": "NOM",
          "direction": "down",
          "confidence": 0.8
        }
      ],
      "sketch": "Create a `ReportFormatter` class that holds `_width` and contains `title()` and `describe_customer()`. `ReportView` would then delegate these calls to the formatter. This separates the presentation concern (width, string manipulation) from the data access concern (repository, mailer), reducing the connected components in the method-attribute graph of `ReportView`."
    },
    {
      "title": "Move customer description logic to Customer or a CustomerPresenter",
      "rationale_metric_link": [
        "LCOM4",
        "DCC"
      ],
      "expected_effect": [
        {
          "metric": "LCOM4",
          "direction": "down",
          "confidence": 0.8
        },
        {
          "metric": "DCC",
          "direction": "same",
          "confidence": 0.5
        }
      ],
      "sketch": "The method `describe_customer` accesses `customer.customer_id()`, `customer.name()`, `customer.email()`, and `customer.tier()`. This is feature envy. Move this logic to the `Customer` class (e.g., `customer.to_report_string(width)`) or a dedicated `CustomerPresenter`. This reduces the dependency of `ReportView` on the internal structure of `Customer` and improves cohesion by placing the logic where the data resides."
    }
  ],
  "risk_notes": "Extracting the formatter increases the number of classes in the system (DCC up) and may introduce indirection. Moving logic to `Customer` requires ensuring that `Customer` does not become a god object if other similar presentation logic is added. The current `ReportView` is small (NOM=4), so over-engineering should be avoided; these changes are primarily for clarity and cohesion rather than performance."
}
